MATHEMATICAL ISSUES FOUND
=========================
1. Hamiltonian convention and complex drive
   The original Hamiltonian used F(a+a^dagger) and declared F real only after a
   phase rotation.  This obscured the self-adjoint convention requested for a
   general complex drive.  For complex F, that expression is not symmetric.
   The model is now written

       H = omega_0 N + (V/2) N(N-1) + F a^dagger + conjugate(F) a,

   with omega_0 real, V>0 and F complex.  A mode phase rotation may still make
   F real and non-negative.  The sign of omega_0 is part of the convention, so
   both common detuning signs are covered by arbitrary real omega_0.

2. Bargmann differential equation
   The original equation was correct only for its restricted real-drive
   convention.  Under a^dagger -> z and a -> d/dz, the corrected equation is

       (V/2) z^2 psi'' + (omega_0 z + conjugate(F)) psi'
       + (F z - E) psi = 0.

   The conjugation of the coefficient multiplying psi' is essential.

3. Coefficient recurrence
   For psi(z)=sum_{n>=0} c_n z^n and c_{-1}=0, coefficient matching gives

       conjugate(F)(n+1)c_{n+1}
       + [(V/2)n(n-1)+omega_0 n-E]c_n + F c_{n-1}=0.

   The manuscript now contains this recurrence.  The exceptional case F=0 is
   explicitly separated; it is diagonal in the number basis and cannot be
   handled by dividing a ratio recurrence by F.

4. Operator domains and self-adjointness
   The original manuscript did not specify the Hilbert space or operator
   domains and made no self-adjointness argument.  The revision defines the
   one-mode Fock space l^2(N_0), the finite-particle subspace D_fin, and

       D(H_0)=D(N^2)={u: sum n^4 |u_n|^2 < infinity}.

   H_0 is the real diagonal operator omega_0 N+(V/2)N(N-1), self-adjoint on
   D(N^2), and D_fin is a core.

5. Kato–Rellich applicability
   The estimates ||a u||^2=<u,Nu> and
   ||a^dagger u||^2=<u,(N+1)u>, together with the quadratic growth of H_0,
   show that the drive is infinitesimally H_0-bounded.  Thus Kato–Rellich
   applies with relative bound zero and yields self-adjointness of H on the
   unchanged domain D(N^2).

6. Essential self-adjointness on D_fin
   This was absent.  The graph norms of H_0 and N^2 are equivalent, and the
   infinitesimal perturbation makes the graph norms of H and H_0 equivalent.
   Hence the H_0-core D_fin remains an H-core, so H restricted to D_fin is
   essentially self-adjoint.

7. Lower boundedness
   No lower-bound statement or proof was present.  For V>0 and arbitrary real
   omega_0, the diagonal polynomial has positive quadratic leading term.  The
   linear drive is absorbed by an arbitrarily small multiple of N^2 plus a
   constant.  The displayed form estimates in the manuscript imply that H is
   bounded below for all F in C.

8. Compact resolvent and discrete spectrum
   These claims were absent.  The diagonal resolvent of H_0 is compact because
   its entries tend to zero.  At a sufficiently negative common resolvent
   point, the factorized resolvent identity combines this compact resolvent
   with the bounded factor [I+B(H_0-z)^{-1}]^{-1}.  Therefore H has compact
   resolvent.  Since H is also self-adjoint and bounded below, its spectrum
   consists of finite-multiplicity eigenvalues accumulating only at +infinity.

9. Entire solutions versus Bargmann–Fock vectors
   The original text stated the Bargmann norm but did not prevent an inference
   from entireness to physical admissibility.  The revision says explicitly
   that an entire ODE solution lies in Bargmann–Fock space exactly when
   sum n! |c_n|^2 is finite, and is an operator eigenvector only when it is in
   D(N^2) and satisfies the equation in the Hilbert-space sense.  Entireness
   alone supplies neither condition.

10. Premature spectral claims
    The original spectral section asserted, without proof, that Bargmann
    normalizability selects a minimal recurrence solution and that a spectral
    function can be constructed whose zeros are precisely the eigenvalues.
    These implications require asymptotic recurrence analysis and convergence
    results not yet present.  They were replaced by conditional, prospective
    language.  No characteristic function is defined at this stage.

CORRECTIONS APPLIED
===================
Only manuscript/manuscript.tex was changed.  The correction:
  - adopts the self-adjoint complex-drive convention;
  - updates the Bargmann ODE and coefficient recurrence;
  - defines the Fock-space realization and exact operator domain;
  - supplies concise Kato–Rellich, core, lower-bound, and compact-resolvent
    arguments;
  - distinguishes entire ODE solutions from Bargmann vectors and domain
    vectors;
  - treats F=0 separately;
  - replaces unjustified spectral assertions with the precise open task.

